  "findings": [
    {
      "finding": "The numeric runtime defect is real and independently reproducible.",
      "evidence": "The old saved formula was correct. Replaying it with only the float representation changed fixes its results. A new Terra trial still fails when only that normalization is disabled.",
      "implication": "Keep the narrow runtime repair as part of the baseline; do not attribute those numeric failures to historical state retrieval."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Correct first answers do not establish reusable state.",
      "evidence": "All three Luna Kiva answers pass, but none retains the full 727-row scored population as an operator result. The first two also hide the cleaned loan table inside their Process callback. The third retains cleaned loans but truncates the pressure result. Luna Restaurants r3 similarly saves only the top eight watch rows.",
      "implication": "Historical retrieval can read saved outputs. Recovering these hidden intermediates requires editing and recomputing code. The missing tables were never materialized as graph outputs."
    },
    {
      "finding": "The existing prompt already contains the intended structural rules.",
      "evidence": "Actual requests include One operator, one operation, Use predefined operators, and the instruction to keep full reusable populations separate from limited reports.",
      "implication": "This is a compliance/salience problem. Adding more catalogue concepts or repeating the same instruction is not established as a solution."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Terra preserves useful state while maintaining lower measured cost.",
      "evidence": "All six Terra Dataflow trials and all six Terra DSGym trials pass. Full scored populations match the audited reference fields in both agents. Dataflow uses 22 calls and 22,681 output tokens versus 35 successful calls and 53,567 output tokens for DSGym, plus three timed-out DSGym attempts.",
      "implication": "This subset supports the feasibility of structured reusable computation at comparable cost; it does not establish a multitask retrieval gain."
    },
    {
      "finding": "The fresh-question script failures are visible in the traces.",
      "evidence": "Four Luna script trials return without executing Python. One Kiva trial reverses percentile direction, percentile-ranks the raw completion gap, and repairs weight scaling without fixing those earlier mistakes. The complete original task instructions remain available.",
      "implication": "These are neither cascades from an earlier task nor explained by missing historical state. The labels agree with direct trace inspection."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Original accuracy/error labels miss successful-answer state defects.",
      "evidence": "Original E.3 classifies failed answers only, so it does not label the four passing Luna Dataflows with incomplete materialized state. A Restaurants answer also reports 44 eligible units despite 70 in its saved graph, while its required top-eight table is correct and receives a pass.",
      "implication": "Retain original scores and labels, and add deterministic state availability, population membership, field coverage and value checks. Treat the ancillary 44-versus-70 claim separately from the required answer fields."
    },
    {
      "finding": "The original DSGym text protocol can waste generated output.",
      "evidence": "Nine Terra responses contain multiple Python blocks. The upstream environment executes the first block and resumes after its observation; later generated blocks and information sections do not represent executed work.",
      "implication": "A separately labeled stop-at-end-of-Python protocol control could reduce script cost. Preserve this campaign as the original-agent baseline."
    }
  ],
